          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/philosophy+of+science"} philosophy of science
            and particularly the
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/philosophy+of+physics"} philosophy of physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/philosophy"} philosophical
            sentiment which expresses the following perspective on the description of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            might deserve to be called
            %em exceptional naturalism
            or similar:
          %blockquote
            %p
              Since nature (reality)
              = succeed "," do
                %em is exceptional in that it has existence
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exceptional+structures"} exceptional structures
              among all
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+structures"} mathematical
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structures"} structures
              — such as the exceptional examples in the classification of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simple+Lie+groups"} simple Lie groups
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exceptional+Lie+groups"} exceptional Lie groups
              — that play a role in the mathematical description of nature, hence in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
              and specifically in
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phenomenology"} phenomenology
          %p
            In that this perspective provides
            %em a priori
            grounds for the way the universe is, it may be contrasted with
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empiricism"} empiricism
          %h2#sources Sources
          %p Sources where a sentiment of exceptional naturalism has been expressed include the following:
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Olive"} David Olive
            (in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#OliveLand89"} Olive &amp; Landsberg 1989
          %blockquote
            %p The results… add the stunning new question as to why nature has an apparent predilection for exceptional structures such as the E groups.
          %p and:
          %blockquote
            %p I sense that the trail of exceptional structures is growing even warmer.
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Baez95"}> Baez 95
            , in a context of the role of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monster+group"} monster group
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
          %blockquote
            %p one may argue that the theory of our universe must be incredibly special, since out of all the theories we can write down, just this one describes the universe that actually exists. All sorts of simpler universes apparently don’t exist. So maybe the theory of the universe needs to use special, “exceptional” mathematics for some reason, even though it’s complicated
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Ramond01"} Ramond 01
            writes, in a context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+string+theory"} heterotic string theory
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
          %blockquote
            %p Nature relishes unique mathematical structures.
          %p and:
          %blockquote
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exceptional+Lie+algebra"} Exceptional Algebras
              are most unique and beautiful among
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie Algebras
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Witten02a"}> Witten 02a
            , in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory+on+G%E2%82%82-manifolds"} M-theory on G₂-manifolds
            says, with regard to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exceptional+Lie+group"} exceptional Lie group
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G%E2%82%82"} G₂
          %blockquote
            %p
              …arise in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-compactification"} compactifying
              from
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} eleven
              to four dimensions on a compact seven-manifold
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G%E2%82%82-manifold"} G₂-holonomy
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Witten02b"}> Witten 02b
            , in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/grand+unified+theory"} grand unified theory
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+string+theory"} heterotic string theory
            says:
          %blockquote
            %p
              Describing nature by a group taken from an infinite family does raise an obvious question – why this group and not another? In addition to the three infinite families, there are five
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exceptional+Lie+groups"} exceptional Lie groups
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G%E2%82%82"} G₂
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F%E2%82%84"} F₄
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E%E2%82%86"} E₆
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E%E2%82%87"} E₇
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E%E2%82%88"} E₈
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Ramond03"} Ramond 03
            writes in a context of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/grand+unification"} grand unification
          %blockquote
            %p
              In the Lie garden, one also finds five rare flowers, the
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exceptional+Lie+algebras"} exceptional algebras
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G%E2%82%82"} G₂
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F%E2%82%84"} F₄
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E%E2%82%86"} E₆
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E%E2%82%87"} E₇
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E%E2%82%88"} E₈
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Boya03"} Boya 03
            writes in view of the various occurrences of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/octonions"} octonions
            in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
          %blockquote
            %p
              If the current
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
              is a
              = succeed "," do
                %em unique theory
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/octonions"} octonions
              %em theory-of-everything
              should display geometric and algebraic structures derived from this unique non-associative
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normed+division+algebra"} division algebra
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Toppan03"} Toppan 03
            writes:
          %blockquote
            %p
              There is a growing interest in the logical possibility that
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exceptional+mathematical+structures"} exceptional mathematical structures
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exceptional+Lie+algebra"}> exceptional Lie and superLie algebras
              , the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exceptional+Jordan+algebra"} exceptional Jordan algebra
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+of+everything"} Theory Of Everything
              (TOE). The maximal
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/division+algebra"} division algebra
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/octonions"} octonions
              can be held as the mathematical responsible for the existence of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exceptional+structures"} exceptional structures
              mentioned above.
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Moore14"} Moore 14
            writes, in a survey of the state of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+physics"} mathematical physics
            applied to fundamental
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/high+energy+physics"} high energy physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle+physics"} particle physics
            (“Physical Mathematics”):
          %blockquote
            %p
              it must be said that much of Physical Mathematics has a predilection for special, sporadic, and exceptional structures.
              = succeed "\u2026" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              I cannot forecast what stormy weather our field is destined to endure, but I can confidently forecast abundant
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moonshine"} moonshine
              in the years ahead. (section 11 “Exceptional structures”)
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Vafa15"} Vafa 15, slide 11
            makes a related comment – in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F-theory"} F-theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/GUT"} GUT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phenomenology"} phenomenology
            requiring a point with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ADE+classification"} E-type
            symmetry:
          %blockquote
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/landscape+of+string+theory+vacua"} landscape
              concept typically goes against things being exceptional. Here we seem to have evidence to the contrary.
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Penrose15"}> Penrose 2015
            , thinking of
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twistor+space"} twistor space
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kmYfYOW0vSg"} here
          %p
            %a{:href => "#HarveyMoore20"} Harvey &amp; Moore 2020
            write:
          %blockquote
            %p
              Physicists who wonder about the ultimate structure of our universe can be divided into two classes. The first class believes that our universe is chosen at random from a huge ensemble of possible universes. The second class believes that our universe and the fundamental laws that govern it are based on some beautiful and exceptional mathematical structure.
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} String theory
              provides evidence for both points of view… The authors of the present paper are philosophically inclined towards the point of view of the second class of physicists and are thus interested in studying special points in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/landscape+of+string+theory+vacua"} landscape of string compactifications
              that involve beautiful and exceptional mathematical structures. Certainly among these special points the ones associated to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moonshine"} moonshine
              are amongst the most beautiful.
          %p
